  <data name="OtherWhyTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>How Spatia reaches every app</value></data>
  <data name="OtherWhy" xml:space="preserve"><value>Windows only lets an app change other apps' sound through an audio processing object (APO) installed on the playback device. Spatia uses Equalizer APO, a free, open-source APO used by millions of people, and writes its settings to a file Equalizer APO reads. YouTube, Netflix, Spotify, games and every other app are then processed.</value></data>
  <data name="OtherNextTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Setting it up</value></data>
  <data name="OtherNext" xml:space="preserve"><value>1. Install Equalizer APO from its official page (Home → Download Equalizer APO).
2. In its Device Selector, tick your speakers and headphones, then restart Windows.
3. In Spatia, choose Connect on Home. Windows asks for permission once, so Spatia can add one line to Equalizer APO's config.txt (a backup is kept) and write its own settings file.</value></data>
  <data name="OtherNowTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Good to know</value></data>
  <data name="OtherNow" xml:space="preserve"><value>Changes apply within a moment, while audio keeps playing. Turn Spatia off on Home to hear your apps unchanged. Disconnect removes Spatia's line from config.txt again. Night compression and loudness normalisation are approximated, because Equalizer APO has no dynamics processing.</value></data>
